From 3b8332ea5d0c615d5a35a668aed7717a699a619a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "David C. Manuelda" <StormByte@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:28:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] libvmaf/feature: rename static close() callbacks to
 close_fex()

Thirteen feature extractors define their close callback as

    static int close(VmafFeatureExtractor *fex)

That name shadows close(2) from <unistd.h>. In C, a static function is
private to its translation unit. LTO toolchains do not always keep it
that way. When libvmaf is linked into an application with LTO together
with other code that calls close(fd), Apple's ld64 has bound those
close(fd) calls to one of these static functions. The file descriptor
is then used as a VmafFeatureExtractor pointer. The first thing every
one of these callbacks does is load fex->priv, which is at offset 0x38,
so the process dies with EXC_BAD_ACCESS at fd + 0x38 (0x3b for fd 3).

We hit this in a macOS arm64 build with Apple Clang and -flto that
statically links libvmaf with FFmpeg. The crash was in
libavformat's file_close() during avformat_close_input(). It goes away
without LTO, and the same build works on Linux and Windows. No LTO
mode is needed to reproduce it on its own; the only requirement is
that libvmaf's bitcode and a close(fd) caller end up in the same LTO
link. FFmpeg removed the same pattern from its parsers for the same
reason (584c41a4ac, FFV1 parser, and the APV parser after it).

This change renames the callbacks:

  - close() -> close_fex() in ciede.c, float_adm.c, float_ansnr.c,
    float_moment.c, float_motion.c, float_ms_ssim.c, float_psnr.c,
    float_ssim.c, float_vif.c, integer_adm.c, integer_ssim.c and
    integer_vif.c. integer_motion.c already uses this name.
  - close() -> close_temporal() in speed.c. It sits next to
    close_chroma() in the same file.

This is not an API or ABI change. The functions are static, nothing
outside their own file refers to them by name, and they are still
reached only through VmafFeatureExtractor.close. No public header,
exported symbol or struct layout changes, and runtime behaviour is
the same. The patch only removes the libc name from libvmaf's
internal symbols. After it, the library defines no symbol named
close.

Signed-off-by: David C. Manuelda <StormByte@gmail.com>
